<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation on hierarchical pre-training levels.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4 Ablation studies ‣ 5 Experimental Validation ‣ SurgLaVi: Large-Scale Hierarchical Dataset for Surgical Vision-Language Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, joint multi-level pre-training (task + step + phase) achieves the best overall zero-shot performance across datasets, confirming that integrating multiple temporal granularities provides complementary supervision. When trained on individual levels, task-only and phase-only models generally outperform phase-only pre-training. We attribute this to two factors: (1) phase-level clips are longer in duration, resulting in fewer training samples compared to finer granularities, and (2) phase captions are considerably more complex, making them harder to learn in isolation. In contrast, finer-grained levels (task, step) increase the number of available samples and provide simpler, more localized procedural descriptions, which may be easier for the model to align with visual patterns compared to phase-level samples. We hypothesize that when all levels are used jointly, the model benefits from the abundance and simplicity of fine-grained supervision and the richer procedural semantics of phase-level descriptions, enabling it to learn robust representations across multiple temporal scales and semantic granularities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="S5.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study of our pipeline components.</span> We pre-train our model on different dataset versions as we apply the stages of our data curation pipeline: Multi-Level Semantic Captioning (MLSC), Dual-Modal Filtering (DMF), and Contextual Enrichment (CE). The checkmark (✓) indicates a component’s inclusion, with the final row showing the performance of our complete method. We show zero-shot F1-score.</figcaption>
